pageType: content
background: { type: solid, color: "$paper" }
notes: |
  Slide 2: Global Detector Network: Coincidence, Sky Coverage, and Baseline Delays.
  - Covers the five terrestrial instruments in the international gravitational-wave network: LIGO Hanford (H1), LIGO Livingston (L1), Virgo (V1), KAGRA (K1), and GEO 600 (G1).
  - Explains the network baseline geometry: light travel times between Hanford and Livingston (10 ms) and transatlantic baselines to Virgo (27 ms) and KAGRA (26-30 ms).
  - Details antenna pattern coverage: interferometers have quadrupolar antenna lobes and cannot point; spatial gaps are covered by orthogonal orientations across the globe.
  - Reviews network duty cycle: simultaneous multi-detector coincidence (joint up-time ~53%) is essential for rejecting local non-Gaussian instrumental glitches.
